1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:06,163
In the last lesson you saw the
agent loop: decide, act, observe, repeat.

2
00:00:06,513 --> 00:00:08,449
Now let's open an agent up.

3
00:00:08,799 --> 00:00:14,520
Every agent, from a tiny calculator to
a big coding assistant, is built from the

4
00:00:14,520 --> 00:00:15,664
same four parts.

5
00:00:16,014 --> 00:00:20,752
Once you can name them, any agent
becomes much easier to understand.

6
00:00:22,552 --> 00:00:25,970
In this lesson, you'll
name the four parts of an agent.

7
00:00:26,570 --> 00:00:31,679
You'll understand what a tool is, and,
very importantly, who actually runs it.

8
00:00:32,279 --> 00:00:35,682
You'll read a tool
definition, and a real tool call.

9
00:00:36,282 --> 00:00:38,677
And you'll see where
the agent keeps its state.

10
00:00:40,477 --> 00:00:41,814
Part one: the model.

11
00:00:42,164 --> 00:00:46,042
It reads everything so far
and decides the next action.

12
00:00:46,392 --> 00:00:47,875
It's the decision maker.

13
00:00:48,475 --> 00:00:50,177
Part two: the instructions.

14
00:00:50,527 --> 00:00:53,309
That's the system
prompt you know from Module 2.

15
00:00:53,659 --> 00:00:56,961
It sets the goal, the
rules, and the style of the answer.

16
00:00:57,561 --> 00:00:59,106
Part three: the tools.

17
00:00:59,455 --> 00:01:03,860
These are functions in your
program that the model can ask you to run.

18
00:01:04,210 --> 00:01:07,008
Without tools, a model can only talk.

19
00:01:07,608 --> 00:01:09,202
And part four: the state.

20
00:01:09,552 --> 00:01:15,038
That's the list of messages: the
question, every action the model took, and every

21
00:01:15,038 --> 00:01:16,325
result it got back.

22
00:01:18,125 --> 00:01:24,106
Let's slow down on tools, because this
is the idea most beginners find surprising.

23
00:01:24,456 --> 00:01:27,122
A language model can only produce text.

24
00:01:27,472 --> 00:01:33,285
It can't run code, it can't open your
files, and it can't browse the web by itself.

25
00:01:33,885 --> 00:01:38,090
A tool is just a normal
Python function, in your program.

26
00:01:38,440 --> 00:01:41,976
For our calculator, it's
a function called calculate.

27
00:01:42,576 --> 00:01:48,276
You describe that function to the model:
its name, what it does, and what inputs it

28
00:01:48,276 --> 00:01:48,688
needs.

29
00:01:49,288 --> 00:01:54,708
Then the model can ask for the tool to
be called, and your code decides to run it,

30
00:01:54,708 --> 00:01:56,426
and sends back the result.

31
00:01:56,776 --> 00:01:59,790
The model never
touches your computer directly.

32
00:02:00,140 --> 00:02:01,586
You stay in control.

33
00:02:03,386 --> 00:02:06,845
Here's how we describe the
calculate tool to the model.

34
00:02:07,195 --> 00:02:08,271
First, a name.

35
00:02:08,621 --> 00:02:11,891
The model uses this name
when it asks for the tool.

36
00:02:12,491 --> 00:02:14,915
Then a description, written for the model.

37
00:02:15,265 --> 00:02:20,465
It says what the tool does, what it
accepts, what it returns, and what happens when

38
00:02:20,465 --> 00:02:21,781
something goes wrong.

39
00:02:22,131 --> 00:02:27,828
A clear description is the main way the
model knows when, and how, to use a tool.

40
00:02:28,428 --> 00:02:29,902
Then the input schema.

41
00:02:30,252 --> 00:02:32,438
You've seen schemas in Module 3.

42
00:02:32,788 --> 00:02:37,683
This one says the tool takes one
input, called expression, and it's a string.

43
00:02:38,033 --> 00:02:41,023
It's required, and no
other inputs are allowed.

44
00:02:41,623 --> 00:02:47,483
And strict true asks the API to make
sure the model's input matches this schema

45
00:02:47,483 --> 00:02:48,077
exactly.

46
00:02:49,877 --> 00:02:53,887
Now let's see what comes back
when the model wants to use a tool.

47
00:02:54,237 --> 00:02:58,687
This script sends one question,
with the tool definitions attached.

48
00:02:59,287 --> 00:03:05,088
Remember, this recording uses the
offline fake API, so a script plays the model's

49
00:03:05,088 --> 00:03:05,446
part.

50
00:03:05,796 --> 00:03:10,128
The shape of the reply is
exactly what the real API sends.

51
00:03:10,478 --> 00:03:15,459
The stop reason is tool use: the
model stopped because it wants a tool.

52
00:03:15,809 --> 00:03:21,451
The block's type is tool use, the name
is calculate, the input is the expression,

53
00:03:21,451 --> 00:03:24,794
and there's an ID,
which we'll need in a moment.

54
00:03:26,594 --> 00:03:28,186
So here's the full picture.

55
00:03:28,536 --> 00:03:33,182
The model writes a request: please
call calculate with this expression.

56
00:03:33,532 --> 00:03:34,726
That's all it does.

57
00:03:35,326 --> 00:03:39,823
Your program reads that request
and runs the real Python function.

58
00:03:40,173 --> 00:03:43,754
This is where you can check
the request first, or refuse it.

59
00:03:44,354 --> 00:03:48,870
And your program sends the result
back to the model, so it can continue.

60
00:03:50,670 --> 00:03:52,297
Part two, the instructions.

61
00:03:52,647 --> 00:03:55,481
Notice we use everything from Module 3.

62
00:03:55,831 --> 00:03:58,735
First, context: who
this helps, and with what.

63
00:03:59,335 --> 00:04:04,699
Then the rules, each with a reason:
work numbers out with the tools, because the

64
00:04:04,699 --> 00:04:05,772
tools are exact.

65
00:04:06,122 --> 00:04:10,364
Models can make arithmetic
mistakes, but a calculator can't.

66
00:04:10,714 --> 00:04:15,002
And if a tool returns an
error, fix the input and try again.

67
00:04:15,602 --> 00:04:20,664
Finally, the shape of the answer: one
or two sentences, with the number and its

68
00:04:20,664 --> 00:04:20,984
unit.

69
00:04:22,784 --> 00:04:24,305
And part four, the state.

70
00:04:24,655 --> 00:04:29,741
Remember from Module 2: the API
remembers nothing between requests.

71
00:04:30,341 --> 00:04:35,636
So the agent keeps a list of messages:
the question, each tool request from the

72
00:04:35,636 --> 00:04:38,385
model, and each result from your program.

73
00:04:38,985 --> 00:04:44,294
Every time the agent asks the model
to decide, it sends the whole list again.

74
00:04:44,644 --> 00:04:47,692
That's how the model
knows what has already happened.

75
00:04:48,292 --> 00:04:53,419
This means the list grows every
turn, and so does the cost of each request.

76
00:04:53,769 --> 00:04:57,654
That's one reason agents
need limits, which is lesson four.

77
00:04:59,454 --> 00:05:00,334
Your turn.

78
00:05:00,684 --> 00:05:02,664
Imagine a tool called get weather.

79
00:05:03,014 --> 00:05:06,736
Pause the video, and write down
what its description should say.

80
00:05:07,336 --> 00:05:12,310
Think about what it does, what inputs
it needs, what it returns, and how it can

81
00:05:12,310 --> 00:05:12,625
fail.

82
00:05:18,225 --> 00:05:23,147
A good answer: it returns the current
temperature and conditions for a city.

83
00:05:23,497 --> 00:05:24,901
It needs a city name.

84
00:05:25,251 --> 00:05:29,375
It returns the temperature in
Celsius and a short description.

85
00:05:29,725 --> 00:05:32,705
And it returns an error
if the city isn't found.

86
00:05:33,055 --> 00:05:36,571
Notice that's the same
pattern as our calculate tool.

87
00:05:38,371 --> 00:05:39,230
Quick check.

88
00:05:39,580 --> 00:05:44,752
When the agent uses the calculate
tool, who actually runs the calculation?

89
00:05:50,952 --> 00:05:54,076
Your program does,
after the model asks for it.

90
00:05:54,426 --> 00:05:56,651
The model only writes the request.

91
00:05:58,451 --> 00:06:03,555
An agent has four parts: the model,
the instructions, the tools, and the state.

92
00:06:04,155 --> 00:06:05,709
A tool is your function.

93
00:06:06,059 --> 00:06:07,874
The model only asks for it.

94
00:06:08,474 --> 00:06:12,999
A tool definition has a name, a
description, and an input schema.

95
00:06:13,599 --> 00:06:17,629
And the state is the message
list, sent in full every turn.

96
00:06:18,229 --> 00:06:23,863
Next lesson, we connect the four parts
and write the agent loop itself, in about

97
00:06:23,863 --> 00:06:25,483
twenty lines of Python.
